  "live_records": {
    "q-39718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39718",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot 6+(-15)\\cdot 3+28\\cdot 5}{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60-45+140}{36+9+25}b \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{70}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
