    "q-40381": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40381",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}{a}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}{a},$ then $z=$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14121442/assets/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{a}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 7+(-3)(-6)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+18+1}{4+9+9+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{23}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}{a}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}{a},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
